content.text:
The station is still running with all three units online, but they are at 85% load, not the full load gas control wanted through peak.

- **Pressure excursion (about t+7 to t+25):** At 100% on all three units, the discharge header climbed about 3 psig/min to 1096 psig. That was above the unit high-discharge-pressure pre-alarms (about 1037–1041) and the ESD pre-alarm (1064). I cut all units to 80% and told gas control, and pressure came back down without a trip.
- **U2 overheating (t+27):** U2's jacket water temperature rose from 162°F to 183°F in about 8 minutes while U1 and U3 held steady. Cutting U2's load didn't stop the rise, so I stopped it in a controlled way and dispatched the technician urgently.
- **Capacity shortfall:** With U2 down for about 63 minutes, U1 and U3 ran at 100% and the header dropped to about 790 psig. Gas control said capacity was short and repeatedly asked me to keep U2 up. I held off restarting it until the cause was confirmed.
- **U2 restart (t+92):** The technician found a shredded cooler fan belt on U2, replaced it and cleared the unit to restart. I warmed U2 up at 50% load, then took it to 80% and 100%.
- **Full-load limit:** With all three at 100% (about 30 MMscfd), the header rose about 8–10 psig/min again. I trimmed all three to 85% (about 25.5 MMscfd) and told gas control. The header has been stable at about 979 psig, with no active alarms and rod load around 70%.

I made no bypasses, alarm inhibits, shutdown setpoint changes or valve operations.

The shift log is written. For the day shift, it flags:
- why the line can't take full output;
- a check of the other units' cooler fan belts;
- possible freeze damage on U2, whose jacket water read 25°F while stopped;
- U2's jacket temperature, which fell unusually fast after the stop, so the sensor needs a look;
- the ambient temperature sensor, which read 70°F at the start of the shift.
